libsqfs (c) 2010 secunet Security Networks <helge.bahmann@secunet.com>

This is a library for creating squashfs images. It creates images
in the version 4.0 squashfs format, usable with Linux 2.6.29 kernel
and later.

What is squashfs?
-----------------

Squashfs is a compressed read-only file-system created by Philipp
Lougher <phillip@lougher.demon.co.uk>. The format allows tight
packing and aggressive compression of file data and metadata.
Squashfs filesystems can be accessed (like all other filesystems)
after mounting a squashfs image placed inside a block device, i.e.:

	mount -t squashfs /dev/sda1 /mnt

or after placing them into a file:

	mount -o loop -t squashfs image_file /mnt

The in-kernel filesystem driver allows only read-only access to
these filesystems, so the images need to be prepared beforehand.
This can be done through the command-line "mksquashfs" tool also
written by Philipp Lougher or through this library.

For more information on squashfs, see http://squashfs.sourceforge.net/

What is libsqfs?
----------------

Libsqfs is a library that allows programmatic construction of
squashfs images. It provides API functions to compose the desired
filesystem layout from regular files, directories, device nodes and
symlinks. Computations required for composing the final image
are (at the users discretion) parallelized and distributed among
multiple threads to speed up creation of the filesystem image.

Who should use this library?
----------------------------

This library is targeted at developers who need to create squashfs
images and want more precise control over the final image than
offered by the command-line tool. Programmatic construction is
beneficial

- to avoid creating a tree representing the desired layout in a
  local filesystem
- for creating files with attributes not representable in any
  underlying local filesystem (e.g. uid/gid, extended attributes)
- when files are synthesized "on the fly"

The following features offered by mksquashfs are currently missing
form this library:

- appending to existing squashfs images
- representing sparse files

